## Enabling GT911 Touchscreen on Raspberry Pi Zero 2W with Yocto
I purchased a 7.5-inch E-Ink display and a touchscreen to create my own E-paper device based on the Raspberry Pi Zero 2W.  
It's a Goodix product that combines an E-Ink display with a GT911 touchscreen. I bought it without hesitation as it was on sale at a low price on Amazon.  

I'm going to modify the <mark>meta-jooojub-rpi</mark> layer created in a <a href="https://jooojub.github.io/en/posts/content/2026-02-03-post-rpi-zero2-defining-custom-image-and-distro-for-raspberry0pi-zero-2w" target="_blank">previous post</a> to enable the touchscreen and E-Ink display.  

In this post, I'll focus on enabling the GT911 touchscreen first.
### 1. Hardware Setup
The Goodix product I purchased is as follows:  
- <mark>GDEY075T7-T01</mark>: https://www.good-display.com/product/483.html

According to the specification drawing, the touchscreen uses the <mark>GT911</mark> IC and communicates via I2C.  
The E-Ink display uses SPI and appears to require a separate controller.  

<PostImageCard 
src="/assets/posts/embedded/2026-02-04-post-rpi-zero2-enabling-gt911-touchscreen-on-raspberry-pi-zero-2w-with-yocto-1.jpg"
alt="GDEY075T7-T01 Specification Drawing"
/>

Fortunately, I was able to use an E-Ink display controller I already had.  
The issue was the touchscreen; I didn't have a spare connector for the FPC connection, so I had to order one separately online.  

I connected the GPIOs by referring to the pin map, and the final hardware configuration is as follows.  

<PostImageCard
src="/assets/posts/embedded/2026-02-04-post-rpi-zero2-enabling-gt911-touchscreen-on-raspberry-pi-zero-2w-with-yocto-hw.png"
alt="HW Configuration"
/>

I wanted to test it with sample code immediately, but I decided to configure the image with Yocto first.  
### 2. Modifying Yocto Recipes - DT overlay
I discovered an overlay DT in the latest Raspberry Pi kernel source. It seems this can be used to easily enable the touchscreen.  

To apply the desired modifications, copy the goodix-overlay.dts file to an appropriate location within <mark>meta-jooojub-rpi</mark> and configure it to be copied during the Yocto build.  

You can create a .bbappend file to add or remove specific behaviors while keeping the original kernel build intact.  
Add the following file to <mark>meta-jooojub-rpi</mark> to use the goodix overlay.  
```bitbake
{
    "title": "recipes-kernel/linux/linux-raspberrypi_%.bbappend"
}
---
$ mkdir -p recipes-kernel/linux
$ vi recipes-kernel/linux/linux-raspberrypi_%.bbappend

FILESEXTRAPATHS:prepend := "${THISDIR}/files:"

SRC_URI += "\
    file://goodix-overlay.dts \
"

do_configure:prepend() {
// [!code highlight]
    cp ${WORKDIR}/goodix-overlay.dts ${S}/arch/arm/boot/dts/overlays/goodix-overlay.dts
}

IMAGE_BOOT_FILES:append = " goodix.dtbo;overlays/goodix.dtbo"
```
Now, create a files directory, copy <mark>goodix-overlay.dts</mark>, and modify the values as needed.  
I added size-x, size-y, and the actual physical sizes according to my hardware configuration.  
Additionally, since I plan to use the touchscreen vertically, I added inverted-x and swapped-x-y.
```sh
$ mkdir -p recipes-kernel/linux/files
$ vi recipes-kernel/linux/files/goodix-overlay.dts
```
```dt
{
    "title": "recipes-kernel/linux/files/goodix-overlay.dts"
}
---
// Device tree overlay for I2C connected Goodix gt9271 multiple touch controller
/dts-v1/;
/plugin/;

/ {
	compatible = "brcm,bcm2835";

	fragment@0 {
		target = <&gpio>;
		__overlay__ {
			goodix_pins: goodix_pins {
				brcm,pins = <4 17>; // interrupt and reset
				brcm,function = <0 0>; // in
				brcm,pull = <2 2>; // pull-up
			};
		};
	};

	fragment@1 {
		target = <&i2c1>;
		__overlay__ {
			#address-cells = <1>;
			#size-cells = <0>;
			status = "okay";

			gt9271: gt9271@14 {
				compatible = "goodix,gt9271";
				reg = <0x14>;
				pinctrl-names = "default";
				pinctrl-0 = <&goodix_pins>;
				interrupt-parent = <&gpio>;
				interrupts = <4 2>; // high-to-low edge triggered
				irq-gpios = <&gpio 4 0>; // Pin7 on GPIO header
				reset-gpios = <&gpio 17 0>; // Pin11 on GPIO header
// [!code highlight:7]
				touchscreen-size-x = <800>;
				touchscreen-size-y = <480>;
				touchscreen-x-mm = <163>;
				touchscreen-y-mm = <98>;

				touchscreen-inverted-x;
				touchscreen-swapped-x-y;
			};
		};
	};

	__overrides__ {
		interrupt = <&goodix_pins>,"brcm,pins:0",
			<&gt9271>,"interrupts:0",
			<&gt9271>,"irq-gpios:4";
		reset = <&goodix_pins>,"brcm,pins:4",
			<&gt9271>,"reset-gpios:4";
	};
};
```
Now, I need to add dtoverlay=goodix to the Raspberry Pi config. Looking at meta-raspberrypi, it seems this can be configured via RPI_EXTRA_CONFIG.  

These settings can be added to the previously created distro.  
While I'm at it, I'll also add settings for I2C and switch the init manager from the default sysvinit to systemd.  
```diff
{
    "title":"conf/distro/rpi0-2w.conf"
}
---
...
// [!code highlight]
+RPI_EXTRA_CONFIG = "dtoverlay=goodix"
+
+INIT_MANAGER = "systemd"
+
+ENABLE_I2C = "1"
+KERNEL_MODULE_AUTOLOAD:append = " i2c-dev"
```
<AdCard></AdCard>
### 3. Modifying Yocto Recipes - GT911 firmware
After building and booting, I saw that goodix was probed correctly, but a message appeared stating that the firmware was missing.  
```shell
{
    "title": "Attempting to load firmware"
}
---
raspberrypi0-2w-64:~$ dmesg | grep -i goodix
[    6.094799] Goodix-TS 11-0014: supply AVDD28 not found, using dummy regulator
[    6.109713] Goodix-TS 11-0014: supply VDDIO not found, using dummy regulator
[    6.240846] Goodix-TS 11-0014: ID 911, version: 1060
// [!code word:goodix_911_cfg.bin]
// [!code highlight]
[    6.246097] Goodix-TS 11-0014: Direct firmware load for goodix_911_cfg.bin failed with error -2
[    6.265196] input: 11-0014 Goodix Capacitive TouchScreen as /devices/platform/ffffffff.i2c/i2c-11/11-0014/input/input0
```
Looking into the goodix driver code, I found that the 'firmware' refers to the configuration values used by the GT911.
```c
{
    "title": "drivers/input/touchscreen/goodix.c - goodix_config_cb"
}
---
static int goodix_ts_probe(struct i2c_client *client)
{
...
	if (ts->load_cfg_from_disk) {
		/* update device config */
		error = device_property_read_string(&client->dev,
						    "goodix,config-name",
						    &cfg_name);
		if (!error)
			snprintf(ts->cfg_name, sizeof(ts->cfg_name),
				 "goodix/%s", cfg_name);
		else
			snprintf(ts->cfg_name, sizeof(ts->cfg_name),
				 "goodix_%s_cfg.bin", ts->id);

		/* Load firmware using goodix_config_cb */
		error = request_firmware_nowait(THIS_MODULE, true, ts->cfg_name,
						&client->dev, GFP_KERNEL, ts,
                        // [!code word:goodix_config_cb]
						goodix_config_cb); // [!code highlight]
...
}
```
```c
{
    "title": "drivers/input/touchscreen/goodix.c - goodix_config_cb, goodix_send_cfg"
}
---
static void goodix_config_cb(const struct firmware *cfg, void *ctx)
{
...
		/* send device configuration to the firmware */
        // [!code word:goodix_send_cfg]
		error = goodix_send_cfg(ts, cfg->data, cfg->size);
		if (error)
			goto err_release_cfg;
	}
...

int goodix_send_cfg(struct goodix_ts_data *ts, const u8 *cfg, int len)
{
...
    /*
       config_addr = GOODIX_GT9X_REG_CONFIG_DATA
       #define GOODIX_GT9X_REG_CONFIG_DATA		0x8047
    */
   // [!code word:ts->chip->config_addr]
	error = goodix_i2c_write(ts->client, ts->chip->config_addr, cfg, len);
...
```
Referring to the GT911's rather unhelpful manual, I confirmed that 0x8047 corresponds to Configuration Information (R/W).  
<PostImageCard
src="/assets/posts/embedded/2026-02-04-post-rpi-zero2-enabling-gt911-touchscreen-on-raspberry-pi-zero-2w-with-yocto-2.jpg"
alt="GT911 User Manual - Configuration Information"
/>

I had to set up the firmware according to my hardware configuration, but Goodix did not provide any specific firmware.  
With only the insufficient GT911 developer manual and using RAG with an LLM, I had no choice but to create the firmware through meticulous trial and error.  

I then packaged the resulting goodix_911_cfg.bin file so that Yocto copies it to the desired location during the build.  

Creating a package in Yocto is straightforward.  
Just create a .bb file with the desired package name and add it to IMAGE_INSTALL.
```bitbake
{
    "title": "recipes-bsp/goodix-firmware/goodix-firmware.bb"
}
---
$ mkdir -p recipes-bsp/goodix-firmware
$ mkdir -p recipes-bsp/goodix-firmware/files

// goodix_911_cfg.bin must be configured manually according to your hardware
$ mv goodix_911_cfg.bin recipes-bsp/goodix-firmware/files

$ vi recipes-bsp/goodix-firmware/goodix-firmware.bb

SUMMARY = "Goodix GT911 Touchscreen Configuration Firmware"
LICENSE = "CLOSED"

SRC_URI = "file://goodix_911_cfg.bin"

S = "${WORKDIR}"

inherit allarch

// [!code highlight:4]
do_install() {
    install -d ${D}${base_libdir}/firmware
    install -m 0644 ${WORKDIR}/goodix_911_cfg.bin ${D}${base_libdir}/firmware/
}

FILES:${PN} = "${base_libdir}/firmware/goodix_911_cfg.bin"
```
<AdCard></AdCard>
### 4. Modifying Yocto Recipes - INSTALL packages
Finally, modify the image recipe to install the necessary packages.  
I've also added several packages that I expect to need later.
```diff
{
    "title": "recipes-core/images/jooojub-rpi0-2w.bb"
}
---
+IMAGE_INSTALL:append = " \
+    openssh \
+    sudo \
+    i2c-tools \
+    python3 \
+    python3-pip \
+    rpi-gpio \
+    python3-smbus \
+    libgpiod \
+    libgpiod-tools \
+    python3-gpiod \
+    evtest \
+    procps \
// [!code highlight]
+    goodix-firmware \
+"
```
### 5. Build and test
Now, let's build the image and test if the touchscreen works correctly.  
```shell
{
    "title": "Build and flashing to SD Card"
}
---
$ bitbake jooojub-rpi0-2w
$ sudo bmaptool copy ./tmp/deploy/images/raspberrypi0-2w-64/raspberrypi0-2w-64.rootfs.wic.bz2 /dev/sdX
```
After flashing the built image and booting, the previous firmware load failure logs have disappeared.
```shell
{
    "title": "Attempting to load firmware"
}
---
raspberrypi0-2w-64:~$ dmesg | grep -i goodix
[    6.027203] Goodix-TS 11-0014: supply AVDD28 not found, using dummy regulator
[    6.028559] Goodix-TS 11-0014: supply VDDIO not found, using dummy regulator
[    6.129915] Goodix-TS 11-0014: ID 911, version: 1060
[    6.180621] input: 11-0014 Goodix Capacitive TouchScreen as /devices/platform/ffffffff.i2c/i2c-11/11-0014/input/input0
```
Checking with i2c-detect, it seems to have been probed without any issues.  
```shell
